WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.438
- So according to the centers for

00:00:11.438 --> 00:00:13.350
disease control and prevention,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:17.033
one in four U.S. adults
live with a disability.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.515
Among younger adults, we're
talking students here included,

00:00:22.515 --> 00:00:25.140
cognitive disability is most prevalent,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.270
for the others it would
be mobility disability.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.373
But if you think about it, you know,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.782
virtually every single one of
us can expect to experience

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:37.640
some form of disability in our lifetime.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.200
And yet in our society and
societies around the world,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.770
we have still a lot of wide spread

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.330
misunderstandings,
misperceptions, and stigma

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.150
associated with disability

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.430
and those who live with disability.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:53.650
I'm Henry Kwan from the
Yale alumni association,

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:54.700
I'm delighted to welcome all of you

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.916
to our panel discussion today entitled

00:00:56.916 --> 00:00:59.970
DiversAbility Addressing Disability Equity

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.622
Inclusion at Yale and Beyond.

00:01:02.622 --> 00:01:06.120
This program is part of a
series events taking place

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.360
on campus, and off, this
month in observance of

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.640
National Disability Awareness Month.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.640
This particular program
is collaboration between

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.967
the alumni association, the
Office of Diversity Inclusion,

00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:19.050
and DiversAbility at Yale,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.570
which is affinity group on campus

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.420
for staff with disabilities.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.210
I want to take this opportunity
actually thank a few people

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.127
who helped make this program possible.

00:01:27.127 --> 00:01:31.285
First is Melanie Norton who's
co-chair for DAY, Melanie.

00:01:31.285 --> 00:01:33.535
(chuckles)

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.980
We also have Deborah Stanley-McAuley

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.090
who's the senior diversity
officer on campus.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.760
I don't see her but I want
to thank her nonetheless.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.090
And last but not least Miranda Monfilston

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:48.060
of the office of diversity inclusion.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.603
Who actually took care of
most of logistics here.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.730
So we hope that you will find
this discussion informative,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.670
eluding and even perhaps inspirational.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.180
But we're certainly delighted
to bring you this lineup

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:04.650
of speakers that we have here.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.620
So please join me in a warm round

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.518
of applause for our speakers.

00:02:08.518 --> 00:02:11.518
(audience applause)

00:02:15.798 --> 00:02:18.443
- Henry I'd like to get
started with a microphone.

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:21.276
(audience laughs)

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:25.330
Thank you so much good
afternoon everybody,

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.070
my name is Janni Lehrer-Stein

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.110
I am Yale class of 78 Saybrook College,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.870
anybody here from Saybrook?

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.660
Shout it out.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.580
Really, seriously?

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:39.480
I'm from the best residential college

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.450
at Yale Saybrook College.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.870
And it is really wonderful to be home,

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.630
my background is as an attorney,
I'm retired from practice.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.960
I'm also a disability rights advocate

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.430
and a member of the disability community.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.730
As you probably have
guessed from the presence

00:02:56.756 --> 00:03:00.732
of the one who is undisputedly
the most adorable in the room

00:03:00.732 --> 00:03:03.370
my guide dog Shiloh who is at my feet.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.140
My disability is blindness

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.120
and I have the privilege of
being moderator here today

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.330
so we're going to do a little
bit of tactile interaction

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.270
when we get to the Q and A session.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:18.170
Please just roll with me this
is gonna work out just fine.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.039
I had the privilege of serving two terms

00:03:20.039 --> 00:03:22.110
in the Obama administration

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.823
on the National Council of Disability,

00:03:23.823 --> 00:03:27.010
serving as the senior
disability policy advisor

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.110
to the Hillary for America campaign.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:33.520
And I currently am the vice chair finance

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.450
for the DNC Disability
Council as well as a member

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.930
of the National Board of the
Foundation Fighting Blindness,

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.330
and a member of the
National Academy of Sciences

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.449
Engineering and Medicine
forum on aging and disability.

00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:47.470
We'll do some ground
rules as soon as we've got

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.620
everybody introduced and
I'm just so delighted

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.050
you could be with us today.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.017
Sarah, I hope they've got one.

00:03:53.946 --> 00:03:55.968
I'm not giving this up anymore.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.500
(crowd laughs)

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.690
- Hi I'm Dr Sarah Poggi.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.190
I'm here in two capacities.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.790
I'll start with the professional one,

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.310
I'm a graduate of the
Yale School of Medicine

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:11.130
class of 1996 and I'm a
high-risk obstetrician.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.010
I take care of pregnant
women with complications,

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.530
medical complications but
also genetic complications.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:21.603
I do ultrasound and maybe
diagnose babies with problems,

00:04:21.603 --> 00:04:24.743
and also in a consultative role,

00:04:24.743 --> 00:04:29.720
I do medical malpractice
expert witness work on cases

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.770
where a child may have been born with

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.010
complications that are leading
to lifelong disabilities.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.994
However the reason I am here
is more for a personal reason.

00:04:38.994 --> 00:04:43.300
My medical school classmate, Dr Matt Poggi

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.980
and I married in 1995 and two years later,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.960
he was diagnosed with multiple sclerosis.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.880
He ultimately became quadriplegic

00:04:54.765 --> 00:04:58.670
and from C1 down so he was
unable to feed himself,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:03.670
really do any anything except
his cognition was excellent

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.716
and he died about six months ago.

00:05:06.716 --> 00:05:10.380
So I'm here to speak I
guess as a caretaker,

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.660
talk about how it was
raising our two daughters

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.700
and at home and I actually
really happy to say

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:19.500
I have a daughter who's here
now and she is in Saybrook.

00:05:20.568 --> 00:05:21.420
(crowd laughs)

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.610
To your point and I'm
going to end by saying

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.620
how I got on Henry's radar.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.600
There was a veteran's,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.320
Yale veterans event in Washington DC,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.960
and my husband was a navy doctor

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.130
he did not step on a bomb in Fallujah

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.360
but he was a quadriplegic veteran

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.700
and he wanted to go to the event.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.300
And like any good caretaker,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.160
my first question was is it accessible?

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.496
And Henry found out that unfortunately,

00:05:47.496 --> 00:05:50.470
for veterans was on the second floor

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.030
of an inaccessible building.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:57.190
And I just expressed concern,

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.277
and maybe I was a little firm (laughs),

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.480
but I want to credit Henry
with coming back in spades

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.750
by saying wow, you know
we need to do better

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.790
for our our alumni that are disabled

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.283
in the community as a whole, thank you.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.940
- Good morning my name is Jim Conroy.

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.180
I'm here from Pennsylvania

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:25.180
and I was here from 66 to
70 in Silliman college.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:29.113
And I studied brain research
physiological-psychology.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.900
My life got formed right after graduation,

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.187
my first job was a study
for a consulting firm

00:06:38.187 --> 00:06:40.960
on the impact of a brand new law,

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.910
the Developmental
Disabilities Act of 1970.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.200
And my first task was to draw a sample

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.010
of facilities nationwide to study

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:52.210
what would happen as a
result of this new law.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.930
And drawing purely at random,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:55.950
the first place to visit,

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:59.680
I drew a place called Pennhurst
State School and Hospital,

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.242
which is one of the most
notorious institutions

00:07:02.242 --> 00:07:05.230
for people with intellectual
and developmental disabilities

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:06.611
in the world now.

00:07:06.611 --> 00:07:09.550
And going there changed my life.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.350
I was in the midst of
the women's movement,

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:12.350
the anti-war movement,

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.850
I was on a stage on May Day in 1970,

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.260
I was in the thick of
it but when I saw this,

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.356
these inhuman conditions,

00:07:18.356 --> 00:07:20.400
I knew something had to be done.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.363
And I've spent my whole life since then,

00:07:22.363 --> 00:07:24.010
trying to figure out what to do.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.780
But my role was simply scientist.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:29.630
So I thought maybe somebody
who studied the human brain

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.147
could be useful in this field.

00:07:31.147 --> 00:07:34.847
And I went to Pennhurst and found that

00:07:34.847 --> 00:07:36.200
there's a lot to do.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.651
In 1998 I went to Los Angeles
to visit college friends

00:07:40.651 --> 00:07:44.280
and we decided to go
see the Holocaust Center

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.260
in which the first place I knew of,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.010
that hands you an identity card,

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.900
and you go through not knowing
whether you lived or died

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.040
in the Holocaust until the end.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.330
Three weeks later I woke
up back in Philadelphia,

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.050
in the middle of the night saying,

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.130
the disability rights movement
needs a place like that,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.810
a place where we'll never forget,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.110
where people can go by
the millions and learn

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.410
what's been happening and
I'm gonna try to convince

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:13.430
everybody for the rest
of my life probably,

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.033
that we need a place of memory
and learning in Washington.

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:20.320
There are four great civil rights museums,

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.260
one is in progress but
there are three that are

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:28.900
there in the mall now and
we'll be working for a fifth,

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:33.250
the forth well, there's
the Holocaust Memorial

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.693
Museum National and the,

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:39.480
Museum of the American Indian,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.080
the National Museum of American,

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:44.760
African-American History and Culture,

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:46.720
and the fourth is in progress,

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.410
the National Women's History Museum,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.690
which was, a commission
was set up in 2014.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.620
The fifth will be, unnamed,

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:57.480
the National Center or
American Museum on Disability

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.780
Rights in history and
every disability group

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.370
will have to be represented,

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:06.058
it's going to take an easy ten years

00:09:06.058 --> 00:09:10.383
but it'll be a good ten years
to devote to, thank you.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:13.473
- My name is Sarah Scott Change,

00:09:13.473 --> 00:09:16.530
I am the director of student
accessibility services

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.650
here at Yale we're the
office that works with

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.030
all students at the university
that register with us

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.490
and identify as having a disability.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.000
I am not a Yale alum,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.730
but I'm a Jonathan Edwards fellow,

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.193
which counts for something.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:30.670
My background is as an attorney

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.620
but I've spent most of my career

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.740
working in disability issues in higher ed.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:39.200
We work with students in
all issues of accessibility

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.276
here on campus.

00:09:40.276 --> 00:09:43.881
Issues related to academic accommodations

00:09:43.881 --> 00:09:46.870
to ensure that students
can access their courses

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.500
and their materials and
their handouts and readings

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.940
and powerpoints in an accessible way.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.810
We work around issues of housing

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.809
and physical accessibility
of the campus infrastructure

00:09:56.809 --> 00:09:58.880
which in the campus this old

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.290
can be a challenging task sometimes.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:04.110
We work on issues around
transportation and dining.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:07.520
And there's really no edge,

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.882
there's no boundary to what we do.

00:10:09.882 --> 00:10:12.410
If we have a student
that has an accessibility

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.600
concern we just do a lot of creative

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.080
problem-solving to try to
find a way to meet that.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.630
Sometimes we're very successful

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:21.989
and sometimes we're still working on it

00:10:21.989 --> 00:10:25.460
and I'm very honored to
be here on this panel

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:27.163
with such great speakers.

00:10:28.963 --> 00:10:33.048
- I'm Paige Lawrence I'm the
token undergrad on this panel.

00:10:33.048 --> 00:10:34.870
(crowd laughs)

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.330
I'm the president of DEFY

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.140
which is Disability Empowerment for Yale

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:39.770
a student advocacy group.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:42.142
And I run the disability
peer mentor program

00:10:42.142 --> 00:10:44.150
which is in its third year.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.920
We now have 32 mentees we
just got our latest addition

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.920
and we have 11 mentors.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.750
So the program continues to
grow throughout the semester.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:56.780
And I oversee all the matching
of mentors and mentees.

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.810
As DEFY president we you
know work to advocate

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.370
for the disabled community on campus.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.630
We have events to build community,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.595
we do advocacy and awareness work,

00:11:07.595 --> 00:11:09.691
we've worked quite a bit with DAY,

00:11:09.691 --> 00:11:12.750
and I also want to say on
behalf of the students,

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.060
we really appreciate what
student accessibility services

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:18.640
does and how hard Sarah
works for all of us.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:22.820
So about myself and not so much
about the disability groups.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.140
I am in Hopper College
formerly known as Calhoun.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.503
So not favorite (laughs) yay.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:32.510
And I'm a history and
new eastern languages and

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:34.110
civilizations double major,

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.730
studying egyptology in the ladder.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:40.160
I plan to study the history
of disabilities although

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.800
we don't have a separate
disability studies program.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:45.270
So I am sitting
disabilities in the context

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:46.840
of different ancient civilizations

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.137
and pre-modern civilizations.

00:11:49.137 --> 00:11:51.630
I personally got involved with this

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.220
because in my senior year of high school

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.209
I was diagnosed with an immunodeficiency

00:11:56.209 --> 00:12:00.039
which is a type of invisible
illness or chronic disability.

00:12:00.039 --> 00:12:02.620
And as a results you know,

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.440
I've had to navigate the
bureaucracy of Yale Health,

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.410
getting approved for infusions
my freshman year of college,

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:11.290
now I receive monthly infusions
and I have to navigate

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.739
you know, my class schedule
around my infusion schedule

00:12:13.739 --> 00:12:16.150
and it has really changed my life

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.370
and my perspective on you know,

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:19.668
how to stay organized,

00:12:19.668 --> 00:12:23.800
how to deal with different
challenges as they arise.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:26.130
It's totally changed my
perspective and as a result

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.070
I became very involved with
the disability community

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:28.943
on campus.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:32.090
- Thank you everybody.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:34.000
So as I stated previously,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.800
it is my great privilege
to be the moderator

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.130
of the panel this morning.

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:39.120
So we're going to set a
couple of ground rules.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:41.490
First of all even though I am here

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.386
to gently guide the discussion,

00:12:43.386 --> 00:12:46.550
we want to make sure that
everybody's opinions are heard.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.590
And so we're going to reserve some time

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:50.290
for questions and comments

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:52.017
at the end of the session today

00:12:52.017 --> 00:12:54.580
and we encourage everyone to keep track

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.130
of what they would like to say

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.930
so that we have an opportunity to be sure

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.110
that every one of the room is heard.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:04.380
Secondly it is part of my
responsibility as monitor,

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:06.890
to be the time police.

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:09.279
And because we have so much to discuss,

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:13.680
in this very broad topic
of disability, identity,

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.330
inclusion and accessibility,

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:19.290
both in terms of here at Yale
and throughout the nation.

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:23.100
We are going to keep to some
pretty strict time limits.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:26.170
If an answer from anybody
in the room goes to more

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:27.440
than three or four minutes,

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.082
you will probably see me rise,

00:13:29.082 --> 00:13:33.730
and gently stand next to
you or behind your chair,

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.310
that's the first hint.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.490
The second hint is likely
going to be a little tap

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.180
on your shoulder hopefully
I'll be able to find

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.233
it but here's the kicker,

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.110
if you do not desist,

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:48.250
then the final element is on secret signal

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.010
to my four-legged assistant here.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.560
Shilo is gonna kiss you all over your

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.242
face until you stop talking.

00:13:54.242 --> 00:13:55.210
(laughs)

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.730
So with that said and
the ground rules set,

00:13:57.730 --> 00:13:59.811
we have really a lot to discuss
and we're going to dispense

00:13:59.811 --> 00:14:02.940
with the urban legend of age before beauty

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.362
and go right to our current
undergraduate student.

00:14:05.362 --> 00:14:07.597
Paige thank you for being with us

00:14:07.597 --> 00:14:10.090
and I'm wondering if you would
open the discussion with us

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.350
by just talking a little bit about what

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.658
you found the most surprising
thing good and bad,

00:14:15.658 --> 00:14:18.920
when you entered Yale as a
student with a disability.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:20.817
- Okay, thank you.

00:14:20.817 --> 00:14:22.980
So I want to start off by saying that

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.610
when I was a first-year,
cause I'm currently a junior.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.230
When I was a first year,

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:30.001
I was still navigating
the diagnosis process,

00:14:30.001 --> 00:14:33.610
coming to terms with the
fact that I was now disabled,

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:36.370
this was lifelong, I would
have to receive treatment

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:37.740
for the rest of my life,

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.650
it's not something that would just go away

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:41.380
or be a temporary obstacle.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.590
And for the first year
I was sort of in denial

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.900
that this was a part of my life.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.196
I wasn't involved with the
disability community at all

00:14:49.196 --> 00:14:52.410
but then my sophomore year I signed up

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:54.310
for the peer mentor program

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:58.590
and I found a lot of individuals
who you know like me,

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.372
have struggled with this new
identity of being disabled

00:15:01.372 --> 00:15:03.860
or having a disability on this campus,

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.656
and who have faced different challenges.

00:15:05.656 --> 00:15:08.070
And I very quickly started learning about

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:10.308
the other types of disabilities on campus

00:15:10.308 --> 00:15:12.890
and how hidden or visible they are,

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:14.650
and how accessible this campus is

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:18.040
or sometimes the lack
thereof of accessibility.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.730
You know how hard student
accessibility services work,

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.300
what the accommodations are in fact

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:24.380
I just learned last
week that there is a new

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.160
accommodation I could
have taken advantage of

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.410
when I was concussed last year.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.830
So there are all sorts of resources

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:32.430
and I think what surprised me the most

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:36.270
was how decentralized the community and

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:40.067
the resources here are and
how hard it is to navigate

00:15:40.067 --> 00:15:43.124
especially when you weren't
born with a disability.

00:15:43.124 --> 00:15:45.240
Because for some of my peers,

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:47.070
who are born with their disabilities,

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.610
they've navigated their whole
life, it's second nature,

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.920
it's not this new additional challenge,

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.720
whereas for me it was just a whole new

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.660
world of figuring out how
it affected me personally

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.890
and figuring out how to
navigate on this campus

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.440
at the same time.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:04.510
So I found that the
decentralization of resources

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:06.478
was the most surprising
thing on this campus

00:16:06.478 --> 00:16:09.100
but then the most positive surprise

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:12.555
was how strong the community
of disabled students here is

00:16:12.555 --> 00:16:15.560
through groups like DEFY,
the peer mentor group.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:18.500
I had a fantastic peer
mentor my sophomore year,

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:21.420
I she's still one my best
friends, she graduated last year.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.708
And she's the one who started
the peer mentorship program

00:16:23.708 --> 00:16:27.200
just over two and a half years ago now.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:32.070
And the the bond that we built
over the shared experience

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.660
of having a chronic illness

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:35.181
and being disabled on this campus

00:16:35.181 --> 00:16:38.230
is so strong one of the
strongest bonds I think,

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:40.920
you can form is having
the shared experience

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:43.160
of being a disabled student on this campus

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:45.750
even if your disability
is wildly different.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:48.350
It is still such a unifying experience.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.900
So I was pleasantly
surprised by the strength

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.560
of the community and the
passion of the students

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.050
that I've been involved
with on this campus.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.030
- [Janni] Thank you so Sarah Scott Chang,

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:00.440
that kind of leads us
into a lot of directions

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.290
in terms of your very important role

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:06.380
in assisting students at
Yale with disabilities

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:09.690
and also interacting with
the faculty and staff,

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:11.450
some of whom also are dealing

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:13.370
with the challenges of disability.

00:17:13.370 --> 00:17:16.720
We heard talk about the diffuse element

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:17.850
of the disability community,

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:19.524
that's something that permeates

00:17:19.524 --> 00:17:22.390
disability community nationally.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:25.700
And we also heard a little
talk about the accessibility

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.580
issues that are certainly
profound when you're trying

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.460
to fully integrate
students with disabilities.

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:33.410
So could you tell us a little
bit about what your life

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:36.640
has been like assuming
the mantle of the head

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:38.880
of this very important department at Yale,

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.470
and what you have viewed as some of your

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.120
biggest challenges and successes.

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:48.440
- So our office has had
a lot of really kind of

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.440
radical changes over the last year,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.440
that I think worked really
hard to increase our efficiency

00:17:55.930 --> 00:18:00.801
and increase the
accessibility for students.

00:18:00.801 --> 00:18:03.840
We just recently changed
our name about month ago

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.610
from resource office on disabilities

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.800
to student accessibility services

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:09.540
and we have had overwhelmingly

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:12.540
positive feedback on that and I think that

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:17.540
that goes a long way toward
increasing the visibility

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.860
of our office to students
that maybe we're not born

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:24.480
with a disability and are kind
of new to their diagnosis.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:28.200
Also students with mental
health conditions that don't,

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:31.070
the word disability doesn't
really resonate with them

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.330
and it more accurately
defines the work that we do.

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:36.029
We work to create accessibility

00:18:36.029 --> 00:18:39.480
and that's been a really good change.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:43.510
We've increased a lot of
our internal efficiency

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:46.670
in ways that students very much appreciate

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:49.300
kind of brought our practices up to date

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.150
so students don't have to come physically

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:53.060
pick up papers from us every semester

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.510
and then physically take
them to their faculty.

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.990
We've gone digital and
paperless and that's been

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.523
I think a really good change for students.

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:05.310
Some of the challenges that we see is,

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:10.310
I think Yale has a culture
of perfection almost,

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:13.690
where everyone is expected to be perfect

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:15.470
in the thing that they do.

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:18.500
And there's not a lot of grace allowed

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:20.330
for people who are not perfect,

00:19:20.330 --> 00:19:21.680
for people who are struggling,

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:24.520
for people who are still
trying to learn something,

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.730
and that affects the community

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.340
of students with disabilities

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.250
because there is the stigma

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:33.240
that if you have a disability

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.193
that's a weakness, that's a deficiency,

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:40.230
and I wholeheartedly do not believe that

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:43.640
but a lot of people do even
students with disabilities

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.440
have kind of internalized that idea.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:50.280
So I think that there's a
lot of room for the campus

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:53.780
to grow and just be a
little bit more progressive

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.163
in terms of what disability means.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.880
The people who say my
disability is my superpower.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.650
I think there's a lot of value there

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:05.820
and just understanding that people learn

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:07.170
in really different ways,

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:11.430
that taking an exam that's
80 multiple choice questions

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:14.280
and that being the only grade for a course

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.740
might not be the best way to
evaluate a student's knowledge

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:20.390
of material every time.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.780
And just kind of allowing
people to understand

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.420
that students can look a lot of different

00:20:26.420 --> 00:20:29.039
ways and if it doesn't look the way you

00:20:29.039 --> 00:20:31.510
are or the way that you expect

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:33.850
that doesn't mean that
it's necessarily bad

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:35.580
or worse or a weak or less,

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.190
it's just different and that's okay.

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.660
- [Janni] So thank you
so much for that comment

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.310
and we're already starting to see a couple

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.665
of themes emerge here in terms

00:20:43.665 --> 00:20:47.490
of the fact that identity
in the disability community

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:49.400
is something that is
unique to every person

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:51.611
with a disability but also in terms

00:20:51.611 --> 00:20:54.820
of the kinds of expectations and supports

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.600
and now the first discussion of stigma.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.610
I'm gonna skip right over to
you Jim for just one second

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:02.927
because stigma has been raised as an issue

00:21:02.927 --> 00:21:05.033
by Sarah to our other Sarah.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.420
This is I think a rare
privilege on a panel

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:12.200
for us to have someone
who has sat with this

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:16.938
really many many hats and
vistas into the various elements

00:21:16.938 --> 00:21:20.500
of the disability community
both as a family member

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:25.500
who has been so recently involved
in the end-of-life process

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:30.610
and I'm sure everyone here
agrees with me in sending

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:33.480
condolences to you and your
family for your recent loss.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.770
Also as a physician who sees the side

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.733
of diagnosis and treatment.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.760
In this case women with disabilities.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.350
And we're over and I want to
get to this with my question.

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:49.040
A member of the caregiving
and supporter network

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:52.370
that surrounds people with
disabilities if they're fortunate

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:53.470
enough to have families.

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:56.240
So we know 61 million
Americans are challenged

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.640
by disabilities but that
number grows exponentially.

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:02.840
If you consider the family
members who are caregivers

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.300
and loved ones and supporters

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:06.300
and all of the work that they do,

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.690
that is usually largely uncompensated

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:10.230
and hardly ever recognized.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:13.220
So Sarah could you talk
to us a little bit about

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:14.620
what your perspective is.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:17.330
Is part of this kind of shadowy existence

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:19.143
behind the disability community.

00:22:20.906 --> 00:22:24.120
- I'll say I was bone
tired for the four years

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:26.110
that he was quadriplegic.

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:29.190
I was the medical director
of my unit at work,

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:30.850
I did not miss a single day of work

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:34.510
because my income mattered
terribly to pay for his care

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:39.320
and we had basically I was
then the medical director

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:41.630
of a nursing home for one person at home.

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:45.280
And he was taking up to 35 pills a day,

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:49.940
I had six aides you know all
sorts of durable medical goods

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.920
that I was having to keep
track of you know things,

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:54.170
he was admitted to the hospital

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:56.470
I think 29 times in four years.

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:59.720
11 surgeries including an
amputation of his left leg

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:03.003
because of a pressure sore
because we couldn't get aides in

00:23:03.003 --> 00:23:05.290
during a blizzard it was just,

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:07.160
it was a lot and at the same time

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:11.054
I have two girls that are,
one was in seventh grade

00:23:11.054 --> 00:23:13.160
when he became quadriplegic.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:15.050
The other ninth grade, the
girl who's now at Yale.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:17.560
But I became a single parent for them,

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.460
he was of course present to them

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:20.840
and they would watch TV or do,

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:22.770
you know do things like this together.

00:23:22.770 --> 00:23:26.420
But they say mom we were
down to half a parent

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:30.240
because we lost dad and you
spent so much time with him

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.280
that we didn't have much of you.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.630
So I have some guilt right now because,

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:38.130
like I wouldn't have been
able to come up and do this

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:40.980
if he were alive I mean there's
an irony in all of this.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:44.563
Because I always had to
have care for him set up.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:50.350
I just know them because we
had had his diagnosis of MS

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:52.760
early and we were both physicians.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.210
We had healthy incomes and we saved,

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.340
so we were ready for our rainy day.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:59.317
It came earlier than we expected,

00:23:59.317 --> 00:24:02.200
but we we could do it.

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.650
And I just fear for the people
out there that doesn't have

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:08.256
the resources that we did to
navigate the healthcare system

00:24:08.256 --> 00:24:11.325
and have a nest egg to go into because,

00:24:11.325 --> 00:24:16.325
we weren't bankrupt but I
mean we definitely took a hit.

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:19.770
I have, I don't know if any
of you have had someone like

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:21.570
this in the home but I have guilt

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:22.870
because I would get frustrated.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:24.405
I would work all day,

00:24:24.405 --> 00:24:27.070
come home you know, have to cook dinner,

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:28.842
feed him with my hands,

00:24:28.842 --> 00:24:32.790
and sometimes I wasn't the nicest.

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:34.793
And I have a lot of guilt about that.

00:24:36.124 --> 00:24:39.190
I'm trying to think what else to say.

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:41.570
I'm really proud I never put
him in a nursing home though.

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:43.390
I'm so proud of that.

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:45.270
- [Janni] So that's
maybe the best point for

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:46.970
us to jump off and talk about what Jim

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.080
has witnessed in his many years working

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:51.960
with the disability community and also

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:54.450
his aspiration to make sure that there

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:56.940
is Institute of learning where people

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:59.580
can come to understand
the disability community.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.410
We have already heard
many of the major words

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.065
that are associated with our community,

00:25:04.065 --> 00:25:08.860
inclusion, accessibility,
support, caregiving,

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:10.860
frustration, guilt.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:14.840
So assuming that we get
this civil rights museum

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:17.600
up and going, in or around Washington DC,

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:21.010
what is the message that you
would like this institution

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:24.280
to send to people who are
not as engaged as we are

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:26.400
in the disability community?

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:29.529
- oh it's education, it's learning.

00:25:29.529 --> 00:25:32.200
People don't understand, they don't know,

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:33.810
they don't know the
history of disabilities,

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.940
they don't know the pain of exclusion.

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:39.310
And I think if we had a place like the

00:25:39.310 --> 00:25:41.810
National Museum of African-American
history and culture,

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:44.230
where millions of people come through,

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:46.650
it's the most record-breaking thing.

00:25:46.650 --> 00:25:49.628
And as you said 61 million
Americans with disabilities

00:25:49.628 --> 00:25:51.270
and their families.

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:54.160
This museum would be a big step

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.670
in learning to accept and include.

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:58.650
And that's the number one message.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.000
It's not just learning,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:01.560
it's actual action,

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:04.620
which is what the NMAAHC does.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.780
It's it's not just learning about history,

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:09.933
it's taking action and changing attitudes.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:12.800
- [Janni] So you used
one of my favorite words

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:14.520
which is action.

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:17.970
I'm sure everybody who has been
part of the Yale experience

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:21.220
and particularly those of us
who were students here at Yale

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:25.070
have some significant
memory of that aha moment

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:26.810
where a teacher really got through to us

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:28.410
with words that have stayed
with us their whole lives.

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.040
So you just mentioned one.

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:33.640
I was fortunate enough to be
a history student here at Yale

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:35.790
and my senior advisor
was John Morton Blum,

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:39.250
who is one of the iconic
history professors.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:42.870
And in our first meeting
when I went shaking with fear

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:45.180
to talk to him about my proposal

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:49.170
for my senior thesis project,
he sat me down and he said,

00:26:49.170 --> 00:26:50.850
I don't care what you write about,

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:52.800
I don't care how much you research,

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.520
you need to understand
that I will not tolerate

00:26:56.520 --> 00:27:00.010
anything that is not in the active tense.

00:27:00.010 --> 00:27:03.510
No passivity, no past
tense, no subjectives,

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:04.750
forget those rules.

00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:06.980
I only want to hear from
you in the act of tense.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.120
And I think that's what
you're talking about Jim.

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:11.549
So a number of years ago,

00:27:11.549 --> 00:27:14.453
gonna go back to Sarah
Scott Chang over there.

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.520
Yale made a remarkable
statement in announcing

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:23.000
that they were going to
go completely digital,

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:26.732
in order to step towards full inclusion

00:27:26.732 --> 00:27:29.170
for the yellow institution

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:31.460
and for their extended community.

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:35.410
And they are now requiring
that not only everything

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:37.790
on campus but that all of the vendors,

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:40.840
make all of their information
available in digital fashion.

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:43.250
This is good news for
someone like me who is blind.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:45.960
But Sarah I wonder if you
could help us understand

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.640
what that means in terms of
Yale taking on a leadership

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:51.733
role and how that transition
is going currently.

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:56.470
- Yeah It means that a lot
of people are able to access

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.360
a lot of things that
they couldn't otherwise.

00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:01.240
And I think that's a wonderful thing.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:04.110
We have some of our digital
accessibility folks in the room,

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:06.670
Mike Vaughn and Michelle Morgan.

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:09.570
The CTL does a lot of things
working around accessibility

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:11.883
and they've been really amazing partners.

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:16.300
We work with students
for course materials,

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:19.460
we work directly with faculty
so that they can remediate

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:21.060
things on their end.

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:25.120
This can be people who have
been blind throughout their life

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:27.410
and rely on a screen reader like JAWS

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:29.880
that reads everything
they see on their screen.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:32.760
It can also include students
who just got a concussion

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:35.430
and have an intolerance
for looking at screens

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:36.820
for the next three weeks,

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:39.010
and suddenly they're able
to listen to their readings

00:28:39.010 --> 00:28:41.770
instead of visually
scanning it on their laptop.

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:44.990
And it's been really really amazing.

00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:47.120
I've heard from a number of,

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:49.296
outside of the community people, vendors,

00:28:49.296 --> 00:28:54.123
and just other people that
use different parts of Yale,

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.660
that the push toward digital accessibility

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:01.660
has been really helpful
on their end in being able

00:29:01.660 --> 00:29:06.660
to access materials but also
in modeling that kind of best

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:08.883
practice behavior.

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:12.650
- [Janni] So along the lines
of best practice Paige,

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:15.960
I just want to know how that
best practice philosophy

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.830
feels to you as a person
who is engaged as a student.

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:21.620
And I know that the digital
element is not so much

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:24.426
associated with your personal experience

00:29:24.426 --> 00:29:26.040
as a student at Yale,

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:28.290
but can you talk to us a
little bit about what the

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:30.570
disability culture is here at Yale

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:34.410
and how you feel inclusion
and integration exist

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:38.400
or is coming along for the
disability community of students.

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:42.290
- Yeah that's a big question

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.450
that we've been working on lately.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:47.790
How to create this community at Yale

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:49.210
of students with disabilities,

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:51.250
and how to make it more inclusive.

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:54.410
So we've taken certain steps
like becoming affiliated

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:56.860
with cultural centers on campus.

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:57.960
We just affiliated with the

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:00.150
Asian-American Cultural Center on campus,

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:02.090
because we're trying to
be more intersectional

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:03.580
in our outreach.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:05.490
We've worked with other student groups

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:07.860
that are not disability
related inherently,

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:11.203
but that do intersect with disability.

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:14.780
We've worked with
different faculty groups.

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:16.750
We've been reaching out to grad students

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:18.970
and working more with the graduate groups

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:21.880
and helping foster
graduate groups as well.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:26.500
And overall we're just trying
to get everyone involved

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:28.740
in this community in the same room.

00:30:28.740 --> 00:30:32.410
Because we've been so
fragmented empirically

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:34.420
that getting everyone in the same space,

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:36.900
on the same page even just in contact,

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.300
has been huge this year.

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:41.440
A new divinity school group is forming.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:44.991
Currently a new graduate group
is hopefully coming soon.

00:30:44.991 --> 00:30:47.960
There are a lot of new groups you know,

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:50.600
on the rise and working with DEFY,

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:52.520
the disability peer mentor program,

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:54.300
and we're also trying to create you know,

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:56.840
longevity for our groups a lasting impact

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.660
that will last even after I've graduated.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:01.020
You know the founder of the peer mentor

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:03.157
program recently graduated
and handed it off to me

00:31:03.157 --> 00:31:04.740
and we want to make sure that

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:06.827
there continue being these transitions

00:31:06.827 --> 00:31:09.280
and that these programs don't just die off

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:12.120
once we graduate we want there
to be institutional memory

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:15.790
as well and you know another
way that we're working

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:18.190
together as a community is that we pooled

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.270
our collective experiences at Yale

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:22.792
and created a resource
guide to disability at Yale

00:31:22.792 --> 00:31:26.114
it is a 26 page document viewable online

00:31:26.114 --> 00:31:29.520
and for anyone who wants the
link I can give it to you

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:33.150
it's just tinyurl.com/disability@Yale

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:37.230
and it's this complete resource
using all of our experiences

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:41.120
with different types of
disabilities to help others

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:43.909
in our community understand what exists

00:31:43.909 --> 00:31:47.280
and what communities
exists on this campus.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.710
It includes a section about
different student groups

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:51.030
that are involved with disability.

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:53.930
There's you know a club that does ASL now,

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:56.070
there's all these different smaller groups

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:58.620
that are tangentially
involved in the community

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:01.630
and help build and grow
the network and outreach

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:05.659
that we have so we worked
very hard to reach a larger

00:32:05.659 --> 00:32:07.570
portion of the student body,

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:09.070
and I think the peer mentor program

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:12.265
is the single best way in which
we've expanded our outreach.

00:32:12.265 --> 00:32:15.640
And you know, last year
we had 20 some mentors

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:17.550
by the end of the year
this year we're in the 30s

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:20.810
and still growing, our mentees,
and we're still growing.

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:23.486
And I think that that
alone just says a lot about

00:32:23.486 --> 00:32:26.140
how more people are getting
involved in the community,

00:32:26.140 --> 00:32:28.372
more people want to be
involved in the community.

00:32:28.372 --> 00:32:32.170
And I will say also on
the peer mentor forums,

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:35.110
when you fill out a form
to request a mentor,

00:32:35.110 --> 00:32:36.470
there's a question that asks,

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:38.760
how much do each these
things matter to you?

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.920
And one of them is
connection with resources,

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:42.470
one of them is mentorship,

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:44.070
and one of the options is connection

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:45.670
with the broader community of students

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:47.490
with disabilities at Yale.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:50.080
Most of the mentees, when
they fill out that form,

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:52.320
say that that matters the most to them,

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:54.699
connection with the broader
community here at Yale.

00:32:54.699 --> 00:32:58.440
So it's clear that students
here want to be connected

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:00.210
with other students who like them,

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:02.717
have gone through the experience
of being disabled at Yale.

00:33:02.717 --> 00:33:05.260
And people want to be a part

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:07.390
of a larger network here at Yale.

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:10.660
So we're working on building that network

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:13.730
the best we can because
it's clear that students

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:15.550
want to be a part of this network,

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:17.600
and want to be involved in these you know,

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.800
subgroups and this larger community.

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:23.760
So we're doing everything we
can to make this a reality.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:24.870
- [Janni] It certainly
sounds like you are.

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:27.702
So I just want to do one
quick follow up question here.

00:33:27.702 --> 00:33:30.979
It will count into your
rebuttal time don't worry.

00:33:30.979 --> 00:33:31.840
(laughs)

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:34.890
You talked about doing
things the best you can,

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:38.430
and it certainly sounds
like they engaged effort

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:39.948
from the side of the students,

00:33:39.948 --> 00:33:42.980
who are within the disability community,

00:33:42.980 --> 00:33:45.700
is beyond anything I think
that I could possibly

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:48.780
have imagined all those years
ago when I was here at Yale.

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:51.143
But how is it really
in terms of acceptance

00:33:51.143 --> 00:33:55.700
by the non-disabled peers
that you have at Yale?

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.180
Is it possible, for example,
for someone in a wheelchair

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:00.090
to be a member of one
of the singing groups?

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:02.700
Or on the stage at Yale Drama?

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:07.026
How easy is it to form
those personal relationships

00:34:07.026 --> 00:34:09.178
to students with
disabilities date at Yale?

00:34:09.178 --> 00:34:11.440
Just in terms of general acceptance,

00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:13.650
could you give us some kind of a picture?

00:34:13.650 --> 00:34:16.950
- Yeah so not to pull on specific names,

00:34:16.950 --> 00:34:19.280
but the DEFY vice president and treasurer

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:21.020
are currently dating each other.

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:21.853
(laughs)

00:34:21.853 --> 00:34:24.803
So I think people with disabilities
do date on this campus.

00:34:26.200 --> 00:34:27.890
But more broadly speaking,

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:30.500
everyone in DEFY is involved with a lot

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:32.720
of other groups on campus.

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:35.400
In fact the vice president
and treasurer of DEFY

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:37.500
are involved in a production of

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:41.950
The Rocky Horror Picture
Show, I think in two days?

00:34:41.950 --> 00:34:44.437
And so they're going to be
on the stage involved in that

00:34:44.437 --> 00:34:46.660
and our treasurer is going
to be wearing a corset.

00:34:46.660 --> 00:34:48.220
Our treasurer is a dude by the way,

00:34:48.220 --> 00:34:49.610
just throwing that out there.

00:34:49.610 --> 00:34:52.410
So the people in DEFY at the very least,

00:34:52.410 --> 00:34:55.110
I know, are very involved
in lots of student groups.

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:58.360
You've got you know, someone
who's in acapella on campus,

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:00.547
we've got, you know a wide
range of representation

00:35:00.547 --> 00:35:03.450
across the different
student groups that exist.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:06.370
Music, arts, there's athletic involvement,

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.370
we have some club athletes.

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:12.191
So people are really getting
involved on this campus,

00:35:12.191 --> 00:35:14.230
you know, regardless of whether

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:15.660
they have a disability or not.

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:17.950
And it seems that student
groups are generally

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.650
very supportive of having students

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:22.160
with disabilities in those groups.

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:24.400
So I think the culture on campus

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:26.750
is a lack of knowledge,

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:29.033
but a general willingness to support.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:32.160
- [Janni] It sounds like
one of the characteristics

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.410
of Yale students everywhere
so thank you for sharing that

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:37.217
and here's the shout-out
to true love at Yale.

00:35:37.217 --> 00:35:40.420
My husband and I, he was
Davenport class of 78.

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:41.638
We're married now 38 years.

00:35:41.638 --> 00:35:45.810
So there is possibility for
life and marriage after Yale

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.546
even all those years ago.

00:35:47.546 --> 00:35:48.379
(laughs)

00:35:48.379 --> 00:35:51.470
I want to switch back to
Jim for just a moment.

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:55.220
Jim you in the class
of 70, me class of 78,

00:35:55.220 --> 00:36:00.220
we're kind of rising seniors
in that very large perspective

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:02.440
of the journey of life.

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:05.340
What do you see as a potential
for some of us who have

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:07.268
had a lot of experience in this community

00:36:07.268 --> 00:36:11.998
in providing expertise or just association

00:36:11.998 --> 00:36:14.490
with the Yale community here on campus

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:16.150
or throughout the nation?

00:36:16.150 --> 00:36:17.690
- That's a funny question for me,

00:36:17.690 --> 00:36:19.640
I never took a history course in Yale,

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:20.930
I never took an English course,

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:22.160
I never took an art course,

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:23.620
I took all science.

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:26.120
And now I'm 70-years old and history

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:28.083
is suddenly fascinating,

00:36:28.083 --> 00:36:29.269
(audience laughs)

00:36:29.269 --> 00:36:30.490
I don't know what happened.

00:36:30.490 --> 00:36:33.905
But my answer is it's to
provide that sense of,

00:36:33.905 --> 00:36:36.163
what came before history.

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:42.440
I got here in 66 and I've been
wrecking my brains for years.

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:44.940
I can't think of anybody with any kind

00:36:44.940 --> 00:36:47.793
of visible disability at Yale in 1966.

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:52.330
And yet, of course, mental
illness was underneath

00:36:52.330 --> 00:36:55.620
many of our experiences and
some of my friends wound up

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:57.660
in bad places because of it.

00:36:57.660 --> 00:37:00.670
But we have a history of
Yale that goes back further.

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:02.585
The American eugenics society,

00:37:02.585 --> 00:37:07.119
from about 1870 to the
1930 the world was consumed

00:37:07.119 --> 00:37:11.520
with eugenics as a leading
belief in social science.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:14.550
Eugenics from the Greek
meaning good birth,

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:16.395
was the idea of the
people who are different,

00:37:16.395 --> 00:37:18.530
people who were lesser,

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:19.930
than the master race,

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:23.290
should be eliminated from
the gene pool entirely.

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.600
That's eugenics.

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:28.003
The AES was on Elmen Grove,

00:37:28.850 --> 00:37:31.510
the headquarters was at Yale.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:34.290
So our history is long and grim.

00:37:34.290 --> 00:37:38.170
And so really, I think those
of us who have been around

00:37:38.170 --> 00:37:41.830
a long time can tell a
story of tragedy and triumph

00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:44.990
because so much has
changed in our lifetime.

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:49.210
When I graduated, 1970, we
didn't have free appropriate

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:51.540
public education in American schools.

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:53.630
That happened because of a lawsuit

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:56.860
at that same institution,
Pennhurst in 1971,

00:37:56.860 --> 00:37:58.430
called the right to education.

00:37:58.430 --> 00:38:00.770
And it was won because children,

00:38:00.770 --> 00:38:02.500
who were not allowed in public schools,

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:06.520
were being sent to institutions
at roughly adolescence.

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:09.180
The ARC, the Association
then for retarded children,

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:10.090
from Pennsylvania,

00:38:10.090 --> 00:38:12.490
filed suit and won the right to education.

00:38:12.490 --> 00:38:14.600
Four years later it was national law.

00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:17.730
My children went to school
with all kinds of kids

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:19.840
and teachers with disabilities.

00:38:19.840 --> 00:38:22.960
And it's made such a difference
in automatic acceptance

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:25.840
and the feeling of big deal,

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:28.350
that's I think where we want to get to.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:30.870
- [Janni] So that leads us
back to both of our Sarah's.

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:33.440
I think let's start first
with Sarah Scott Chang

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:35.840
and your discussion about the work.

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:37.610
And we know that it's
hard work that you do

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:40.820
in order to ensure that students
with disabilities at Yale,

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:43.700
have the opportunity equally with students

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:46.680
who do not have disabilities to engage

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:50.290
and choose their classes
and really be fully involved

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:51.796
like any other student.

00:38:51.796 --> 00:38:55.960
What we're looking at towards
that goal of full inclusion.

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.710
What would you say we need
to do on Yale's campus,

00:38:59.710 --> 00:39:02.200
in order to get to that next step,

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:04.793
where inclusion is a given,

00:39:06.159 --> 00:39:08.460
where as Paige has told us

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:11.360
it's easy for students with disabilities

00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:13.960
to be in the broader community

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:15.330
for extracurricular activities,

00:39:15.330 --> 00:39:18.530
but also in terms of the
core educational work

00:39:18.530 --> 00:39:19.940
that goes on here.

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:22.340
Classes, studying, examinations.

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:24.083
Could you talk a little bit about that?

00:39:24.083 --> 00:39:26.730
- Like Paige said,

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:29.380
I think that the people at Yale

00:39:29.380 --> 00:39:31.500
want to do the right thing.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:33.180
People want to be inclusive,

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:36.490
people want to be on the
right side of history,

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:38.964
and sometimes they just
don't know how to do that.

00:39:38.964 --> 00:39:43.840
There was an amazing
panel last week with Paige

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.484
and a number of her student colleagues

00:39:46.484 --> 00:39:49.763
on how to build an
inclusive classroom at Yale.

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:54.330
It was great and they talked about,

00:39:54.330 --> 00:39:55.890
from a student's perspective,

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:59.110
what it means to be going to class

00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:02.600
and have some assignment or materials

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:03.880
or whatever it might be

00:40:03.880 --> 00:40:06.130
in your academic program that's there

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:08.290
but you can't quite access it,

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:11.650
because of the way the
professor is presenting it,

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.123
because of the requirements of the course.

00:40:15.013 --> 00:40:19.030
And a lot of that could
be fixed if there was less

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:23.470
of a concern of this is the way we do it

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:24.770
because this is the way we've done it

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.500
for the last 300-years.

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:30.468
And more of a mindset of
how can I make my course

00:40:30.468 --> 00:40:34.180
and this information
available and accessible

00:40:34.180 --> 00:40:37.220
to the most number of students
in the most number of ways.

00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:40.276
Because not everybody learns the same way

00:40:40.276 --> 00:40:43.550
and if the goal of a class
is for students to learn

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:47.770
information and to have
mastery of that information

00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:50.230
there's a lot of different
ways that that can be done.

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:53.280
So I would really love to see more

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:56.300
of an inclusion mindset across campus,

00:40:56.300 --> 00:40:58.700
especially in the faculty
because I think they are

00:40:58.700 --> 00:41:02.520
in a great position of power
and can model that kind

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:04.515
of behavior for everyone.

00:41:04.515 --> 00:41:07.971
Students also, I think
students are naturally,

00:41:07.971 --> 00:41:10.263
tend to be a little more inclusive.

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.630
Faculty, staff, everybody.

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:17.120
Just thinking about how
can we make this thing,

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.420
whether it's a class or a student event,

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:21.490
or a talk like this,

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.373
more accessible to more people.

00:41:24.490 --> 00:41:27.933
- [Janni] So Sarah,
other Sarah, Sarah Poggi.

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:32.820
You've told us with such
great depth of feeling

00:41:32.820 --> 00:41:34.910
about the frustration and the guilt

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:38.180
and also the cost involved
in caring for a loved one,

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:41.734
who is in the final stages
of dealing with a disability.

00:41:41.734 --> 00:41:45.076
We know that this is true
throughout all of the stages

00:41:45.076 --> 00:41:47.810
for a person who is
challenged by disability.

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:49.750
And I'm wondering if you could,

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:53.290
in your capacity as a
professional caregiver,

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:54.440
as a physician,

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:56.981
talk a little bit about
what you think could be done

00:41:56.981 --> 00:42:00.380
in order to ensure
accessibility of medical care,

00:42:00.380 --> 00:42:03.420
both in terms of the
availability of physicians,

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:04.800
physicians with disabilities,

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:07.063
and also the cost issues
that we're facing.

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:12.370
- So I take care of pregnant women

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:16.058
and I have taken care of women
with spinal cord injuries

00:42:16.058 --> 00:42:17.423
who give birth.

00:42:19.382 --> 00:42:22.330
And there's unique
considerations with that,

00:42:22.330 --> 00:42:25.410
in terms of I work in a
hospital so my hospital

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:27.060
is completely accessible,

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:29.170
so that's not been a challenge for me.

00:42:29.170 --> 00:42:32.700
But, may I mention the
discussion we had last night

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:34.550
about the mammograms?

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:36.940
This was actually something
that was brought on my radar

00:42:36.940 --> 00:42:39.030
because it's not something I deal with.

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:41.180
But for example women,
when they have mammograms,

00:42:41.180 --> 00:42:45.140
have to be standing and this is not,

00:42:45.140 --> 00:42:48.160
having a mammogram device
that will accommodate

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:51.630
a woman who is seated is
not readily available.

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:53.940
And so this is the sort of thing that

00:42:55.240 --> 00:42:57.550
needs to be on people's radar because

00:42:57.550 --> 00:42:58.600
just because you're disabled

00:42:58.600 --> 00:43:00.990
doesn't mean you can't get breast cancer.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:03.180
So that would be an example.

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:05.494
What was the follow up of your question?

00:43:05.494 --> 00:43:07.450
- [Janni] I was just hoping
you might also help us

00:43:07.450 --> 00:43:10.230
understand a little bit about the cost.

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:12.320
This is a major discussion

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:13.770
raging across the nation right now

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:16.030
in terms of how we deal
with the availability

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:17.420
and cost of healthcare.

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:19.883
And perhaps you could share
some of your thoughts about

00:43:19.883 --> 00:43:22.560
best ways to wrap the disability community

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:23.970
in a meaningful way.

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:26.330
- Well, everything's getting
dismantled right now,

00:43:26.330 --> 00:43:27.163
it's terrible.

00:43:27.163 --> 00:43:28.300
And I have patients that are,

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:31.420
I mean I practice in Virginia
which is not Mississippi

00:43:31.420 --> 00:43:34.000
but I have patients
that have lost insurance

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:36.500
because of the dismantling of Obamacare.

00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:39.979
And so people will
choose you know they may,

00:43:39.979 --> 00:43:41.790
I'll give an example of a diabetic,

00:43:41.790 --> 00:43:44.440
which isn't necessarily a disabled person

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:46.297
but they'll ration their insulin,

00:43:46.297 --> 00:43:49.365
that puts a baby than at
risk for a stillbirth.

00:43:49.365 --> 00:43:51.990
So I don't know what the solution is,

00:43:51.990 --> 00:43:54.325
I feel like that's way above my pay grade.

00:43:54.325 --> 00:43:56.760
Is there a way I could address those,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:58.779
something he brought up with the eugenics?

00:43:58.779 --> 00:43:59.612
- [Janni] Absolutely, go right ahead.

00:43:59.612 --> 00:44:02.680
- So in my capacity as a high-risk OBGYN,

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:04.090
I feel like almost what you're saying,

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:06.624
what's old is new again.

00:44:06.624 --> 00:44:09.483
ACOG the American College of OBGYN,

00:44:10.360 --> 00:44:13.900
mandates that we offer
all pregnant couples

00:44:13.900 --> 00:44:17.660
screening for 108 genetic diseases.

00:44:17.660 --> 00:44:20.860
And so if a couple finds out
that their child may have this

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:23.540
and additionally we're
doing 18 week ultrasounds

00:44:23.540 --> 00:44:26.300
to look to see if the
babies have birth defects,

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:29.440
the couples are now faced
with a difficult decision.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:33.920
And these are births that would
have happened a generation

00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:36.810
ago without anybody knowing
that they had a child

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:39.100
that might have a particular problem.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:43.740
So I definitely, in the
privacy of my doctor's offices,

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:47.610
and seeing patients make
the decision to terminate,

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:50.240
children that would be
born with disabilities.

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:52.000
And that's a really complicated topic.

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:54.890
It was complicated for me
with a husband who is disabled

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:56.600
that I loved terribly,

00:44:56.600 --> 00:44:58.165
but I have to of course be,

00:44:58.165 --> 00:45:01.160
just provide support for whatever decision

00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:04.420
and be in compliance with the law.

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:06.260
But I think there is a
movement for any of you

00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:07.470
that have been pregnant recently

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:09.880
or have people that are
pregnant in your life,

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:12.283
you may be aware that this is--

00:45:12.283 --> 00:45:13.840
The pendulums kind of
switching a little bit

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:15.120
with the science advancing,

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:17.970
with all the testing that
we can do on fetuses.

00:45:17.970 --> 00:45:19.790
- [Janni] So the bioethics concern,

00:45:19.790 --> 00:45:21.300
for people in the disability community,

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:23.840
is something that has
really been prominent.

00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:27.074
Both in situations like
the one you're describing,

00:45:27.074 --> 00:45:32.074
where people are being offered
or forced to make choices,

00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:35.760
about welcoming someone with a disability

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:37.710
into the world and their family.

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:40.840
And also on the broader
topics that engage all of us,

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:43.996
healthcare, education,
transportation, housing,

00:45:43.996 --> 00:45:46.220
perhaps most prominently in the news,

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:50.067
the discussion about the ethics
around self-driving cars,

00:45:50.067 --> 00:45:52.730
we know that that would
be of tremendous value

00:45:52.730 --> 00:45:54.030
to someone like me who is blind,

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:56.970
I haven't been able to drive
since I was 26-years old.

00:45:56.970 --> 00:46:00.020
And yet there are ethical considerations

00:46:00.020 --> 00:46:02.650
and push backs how these algorithms work,

00:46:02.650 --> 00:46:05.220
in terms of making a
decision about what happens

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:08.240
if a collision has to occur.

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:11.160
Do you select to have less impact

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:13.970
to the car that has a baby
carrier in the back seat?

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:16.360
Do you decide that it's not so important,

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:19.130
if we know that the
driver is over the age of,

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:22.301
well in deference to Jim
and myself might say 85?

00:46:22.301 --> 00:46:24.040
(crowd laughs)

00:46:24.040 --> 00:46:26.550
These are very important
issues that might,

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:29.110
Henry, be the subject
of another discussion

00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:31.570
at the Yale community.

00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:35.310
Or perhaps among the alumni
to talk about the ethics

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:37.650
that are involved in these
very difficult decisions.

00:46:37.650 --> 00:46:40.210
So we want to go to some
Q&amp;A but before we do that

00:46:40.210 --> 00:46:42.320
I'd like to engage with all of you,

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:47.320
in what I like to think of as
last round rapid-fire answer.

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:52.980
So get ready with one sentence or less,

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:54.300
as an answer to this question.

00:46:54.300 --> 00:46:55.250
I'm going to start with Paige

00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:57.540
and just move right across the panel.

00:46:57.540 --> 00:46:59.230
And the question is this,

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:01.750
2020 marks the 30th anniversary

00:47:01.750 --> 00:47:04.680
of the passage of the Americans
with Disabilities Act.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:07.550
We literally have people
sitting at this table,

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:08.852
who lived most of their lives,

00:47:08.852 --> 00:47:10.760
before that became a law,

00:47:10.760 --> 00:47:13.950
and then we have Paige who
has lived her entire life,

00:47:13.950 --> 00:47:17.360
the recipient of the benefits of that law.

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:19.410
So next year in 2020,

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:22.570
but then let's just push it ten years out.

00:47:22.570 --> 00:47:24.380
In one sentence or less,

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:28.469
what do you want to see as
the most important focus

00:47:28.469 --> 00:47:32.383
for the disability and engaged
community going forward?

00:47:33.830 --> 00:47:34.663
Paige?

00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:42.040
- It's rapid fire so in
lieu of a more, you know,

00:47:42.040 --> 00:47:44.160
complicated answer I would
say a really important

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:48.610
element of the disability
conversation going forward

00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:50.853
you know so in ten years from now,

00:47:52.050 --> 00:47:55.560
I would really love to see
the focus of the disability

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:58.890
community shifting from
receiving accommodations,

00:47:58.890 --> 00:48:02.900
to okay everyone's
accommodated the next step

00:48:02.900 --> 00:48:06.710
is building better community
and making it so people

00:48:06.710 --> 00:48:09.630
aren't afraid of disability
and being disabled

00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:11.930
and this also goes back to
the eugenics conversation,

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:15.260
not being afraid to have a
disabled child for example,

00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:16.230
like they are today.

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:19.080
Even with the existing
accommodations it's still so

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.733
stigmatized that people
are afraid of disability.

00:48:22.630 --> 00:48:23.860
- [Janni] Sarah?

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:26.440
- So my answer is kind
of similar to Paige's

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:28.010
and that I would like to see a world,

00:48:28.010 --> 00:48:30.520
where my office didn't need to exist,

00:48:30.520 --> 00:48:33.290
where we didn't need
to make accommodations

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:38.290
because everything was universal
design and accommodations

00:48:38.300 --> 00:48:40.700
weren't needed because everyone
could access everything

00:48:40.700 --> 00:48:43.990
at any time might, be a few
more than 10 years from now.

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:45.160
(crowd laughs)

00:48:45.160 --> 00:48:45.993
- [Janni] Jim?

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:50.690
- On September 6, 2018 the New York Times

00:48:50.690 --> 00:48:52.360
published an article inopted

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:55.720
by five high school students called,

00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:59.330
it's time for a national
museum on disabilities.

00:48:59.330 --> 00:49:00.813
I give us ten years.

00:49:01.784 --> 00:49:02.720
- [Janni] All right.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:03.553
And Sarah?

00:49:03.553 --> 00:49:06.731
This is real simple, don't
park in my handicap spot.

00:49:06.731 --> 00:49:08.790
(crowd laughs)

00:49:08.790 --> 00:49:09.883
When I got the van.

00:49:10.921 --> 00:49:13.940
- [Janni] I echo that sentiment.

00:49:13.940 --> 00:49:16.420
So you know as moderator,

00:49:16.420 --> 00:49:19.500
I just want to first of all
thank all of our panelists,

00:49:19.500 --> 00:49:24.030
for being just really both
attentive to the topic

00:49:24.030 --> 00:49:27.080
of conversation and for
sharing your insight.

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:30.490
I want to answer my own question
in one word in 10 years.

00:49:30.490 --> 00:49:32.808
I want to see us as fully included,

00:49:32.808 --> 00:49:35.610
and I mean fully included.

00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:39.013
So don't tell me you don't
want my dog in your restaurant,

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:42.160
don't kick me out of your Uber,

00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:44.700
don't tell me that I can't
be part of a book club

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:46.300
even though I can no longer read

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:48.270
and I'm technically illiterate.

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:49.380
Just let us in.

00:49:49.380 --> 00:49:51.990
So with that I'm gonna
ask Henry to come back

00:49:51.990 --> 00:49:55.050
and steal my microphone
again so that we can take

00:49:55.050 --> 00:49:56.862
some kinds of questions
- He's right here, oh.

00:49:56.862 --> 00:49:58.820
(distant voice)

00:49:58.820 --> 00:50:00.730
- Oh okay, what we got?

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:03.063
- Oh I'm sorry, guess I got confused.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:05.463
- Any questions?

00:50:08.860 --> 00:50:09.693
- [Richard] That was super.

00:50:09.693 --> 00:50:11.950
I'm Richard Kane, med school 76.

00:50:11.950 --> 00:50:14.910
I'm curious about the
educational piece at Yale.

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:16.880
We have Dean Chun here as well perhaps.

00:50:16.880 --> 00:50:20.270
I'm just wondering if there is some room

00:50:20.270 --> 00:50:22.900
in the curriculum in orientation,

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:25.093
at the med school are now
looking at putting disability

00:50:25.093 --> 00:50:27.600
into the curriculum
there's a tiniest snippet

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:29.070
in orientation.

00:50:29.070 --> 00:50:32.880
As a physician I was never
taught anything about disability,

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:36.370
I merely acquired it by
one-on-one experience.

00:50:36.370 --> 00:50:38.240
Even how to deal with
people in wheelchairs,

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:40.920
there's literally no
teaching in Yale med school,

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:41.753
at this point.

00:50:41.753 --> 00:50:44.755
So that educational piece I
think would be really important,

00:50:44.755 --> 00:50:47.310
not only in the medical school,

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:49.330
but across all of the university.

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:52.180
and I would love to see that
opportunity to be developed.

00:50:54.810 --> 00:50:55.660
- I'll take that.

00:50:56.790 --> 00:51:00.780
Well I can speak to what
we do for education.

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:02.900
I am that little bit of education

00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:04.300
at the med school right now.

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:07.940
I spent the summer going out
to all the different programs,

00:51:07.940 --> 00:51:10.640
orienting the new students
talking about our office

00:51:10.640 --> 00:51:11.473
and what we do,

00:51:11.473 --> 00:51:16.473
reminding the medical students
that the disability exists

00:51:16.480 --> 00:51:20.060
but I can't, I can't provide
the curriculum for them

00:51:20.060 --> 00:51:22.760
and I agree that is something
that would be really great

00:51:22.760 --> 00:51:25.690
because this is something that
as healthcare practitioners,

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:27.820
they're going to be dealing with probably

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:29.963
every day of their lives.

00:51:31.004 --> 00:51:35.190
So we have have worked on
increasing our kind of community

00:51:35.190 --> 00:51:39.160
outreach and getting students
as they come in the door,

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:40.990
to tell them who we are and what we do,

00:51:40.990 --> 00:51:43.040
and not to forget that we exist.

00:51:43.040 --> 00:51:45.491
But I think there's a
lot of room for growth.

00:51:45.491 --> 00:51:47.277
- [Attendee] Yeah I definitely know

00:51:47.277 --> 00:51:49.936
and was actually looking at
those, into the curriculum,

00:51:49.936 --> 00:51:52.450
and the hiring of staff with disabilities

00:51:52.450 --> 00:51:53.640
for including them.

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:54.760
- Oh great.

00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:57.160
- I also want to add from
an undergraduate perspective

00:51:57.160 --> 00:52:00.270
so not necessarily a
medical school perspective,

00:52:00.270 --> 00:52:03.700
but in terms of you know
finding areas and ways

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:05.700
in which to learn about disability,

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:08.680
I found that I've had to
create the curriculum myself

00:52:08.680 --> 00:52:10.510
within existing classes.

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:13.960
So as a history major, studying
history of disabilities,

00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:17.068
I've taken classes on a
random ancient civilizations

00:52:17.068 --> 00:52:20.050
where you know the the whole seminar

00:52:20.050 --> 00:52:21.510
leads up to this term paper

00:52:21.510 --> 00:52:24.493
on whatever topic you choose
within the field of study.

00:52:24.493 --> 00:52:28.430
And I make that topic
paper about disability

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:30.730
in that ancient civilization
or whatever it is.

00:52:30.730 --> 00:52:33.840
I try to apply it and I have to actively

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:36.361
you know, teach myself,
do that own research,

00:52:36.361 --> 00:52:40.628
write the paper myself and
create that disability education

00:52:40.628 --> 00:52:42.980
that I would like to have.

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:44.100
But I have found you know,

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:46.110
every year there's
usually one or two courses

00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:47.890
often in the psychology department,

00:52:47.890 --> 00:52:50.652
that talk about working with
children with disabilities

00:52:50.652 --> 00:52:52.820
and I think that's about the extent

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:55.480
of the undergraduate curriculum
that is generally provided.

00:52:55.480 --> 00:52:58.310
There's also history of
science, history of medicine,

00:52:58.310 --> 00:53:01.790
in public health so HSHM, as a department,

00:53:01.790 --> 00:53:04.580
occasionally talks more to disability

00:53:04.580 --> 00:53:08.090
or mentions it in one
week of the entire class.

00:53:08.090 --> 00:53:11.196
And so there are small
areas of opportunity

00:53:11.196 --> 00:53:15.580
where if you are really looking
for it, you can find it.

00:53:15.580 --> 00:53:18.161
Once per semester, one week per semester,

00:53:18.161 --> 00:53:20.723
but there is no overarching curriculum

00:53:20.723 --> 00:53:23.757
or waiver students not
interested in disability

00:53:23.757 --> 00:53:26.700
to be exposed to it in an academic context

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:28.240
which i think is also very important

00:53:28.240 --> 00:53:30.050
for normalizing disability.

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:32.730
- [Janni] So Paige, Sarah,
just to follow that up.

00:53:32.730 --> 00:53:35.430
Do you think that it would be
reasonable to envision a day

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:40.170
where there is a major
disability rights and advocacy,

00:53:40.170 --> 00:53:42.589
the way we've developed
majors for American studies?

00:53:42.589 --> 00:53:45.840
- So I'll jump in really
quickly and say that

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:47.810
one of DEFY's long-term goals

00:53:47.810 --> 00:53:51.743
is to get some sort of disability
studies program at Yale.

00:53:51.743 --> 00:53:54.400
It's a very long-term
goal I know right now,

00:53:54.400 --> 00:53:56.660
with ethnicity race and migration

00:53:56.660 --> 00:53:59.200
that's been just a whole other topic

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:01.780
about you know getting that
approved as a field of study

00:54:01.780 --> 00:54:04.930
and getting hiring power for the faculty,

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:06.850
and you know we would like ultimately,

00:54:06.850 --> 00:54:09.140
have something similar
where disability studies

00:54:09.140 --> 00:54:11.050
has like one or two faculty members,

00:54:11.050 --> 00:54:12.800
over time even more,

00:54:12.800 --> 00:54:15.273
and that students can study it.

00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:19.220
Studying the history of disability
advocacy things like that

00:54:19.220 --> 00:54:21.270
not just studying in a medical context,

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:23.460
as you know disabled bodies to be studied

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:24.610
from the medical perspective,

00:54:24.610 --> 00:54:26.390
but as disabled individuals,

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:30.030
to understand, to learn about
to learn the history of.

00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:32.860
And again it's one of our ultimate goals.

00:54:32.860 --> 00:54:35.210
So I would like to see some sort of,

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:36.170
not necessarily major,

00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:38.400
but a program of study here at Yale.

00:54:38.400 --> 00:54:40.400
- I completely agree with Paige.

00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:43.430
I think that it would go really really far

00:54:43.430 --> 00:54:47.279
toward building community
and building that inclusive

00:54:47.279 --> 00:54:50.260
ten year from now world,

00:54:50.260 --> 00:54:53.200
where disability is just
another facet of life

00:54:53.200 --> 00:54:56.320
and just another identity
that people can have.

00:54:56.320 --> 00:54:57.950
Your question was whether it's reasonable,

00:54:57.950 --> 00:55:00.150
I'm not sure about that
but I think it would be

00:55:00.150 --> 00:55:00.983
a great thing to do.

00:55:00.983 --> 00:55:02.470
- Well I think in terms of reasonability,

00:55:02.470 --> 00:55:04.010
already we're seeing some support

00:55:04.010 --> 00:55:05.800
from certain departments here at Yale.

00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:08.880
Like women in gender and
sexuality studies, WGSS,

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:09.755
has been pretty supportive

00:55:09.755 --> 00:55:12.370
and willing to potentially

00:55:12.370 --> 00:55:15.290
include some disability
studies in their curriculum.

00:55:15.290 --> 00:55:18.260
So that would be you know,
an avenue for us to pursue.

00:55:18.260 --> 00:55:22.660
Would be starting out by having
disability studies offered

00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:25.210
within a larger studies program,

00:55:25.210 --> 00:55:26.610
and then eventually branching off

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:28.850
into its own studies program.

00:55:28.850 --> 00:55:29.857
- [Janni] So I wanna
ask our friend out here

00:55:29.857 --> 00:55:32.303
in the audience that raised a question.

00:55:33.500 --> 00:55:35.530
What is been your experience,

00:55:35.530 --> 00:55:38.440
in terms of people in
the medical community,

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:42.224
doctors, nurses, assistants,
with disabilities,

00:55:42.224 --> 00:55:46.540
being part of the caregiving community,

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:48.940
and interacting with the patients?

00:55:48.940 --> 00:55:52.168
Have you experienced that and
do you want to see more of it?

00:55:52.168 --> 00:55:55.027
- [Attendee] Oh definitely
and there's a alum,

00:55:55.027 --> 00:55:58.919
I think his name is Ryan
Martin and he is an OBGYN

00:55:58.919 --> 00:56:00.960
who is wheelchair bound,

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:02.353
and he operates,

00:56:03.250 --> 00:56:06.440
he operates doing OBGYN surgery

00:56:06.440 --> 00:56:09.780
using a lift and they
have created a way for him

00:56:09.780 --> 00:56:12.280
to do his practice despite his disability.

00:56:12.280 --> 00:56:15.900
I've had classmates
with minor disabilities,

00:56:15.900 --> 00:56:18.072
but I've also seen people at the school

00:56:18.072 --> 00:56:20.400
who have more significant disability.

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:23.240
And the hope is that they
can continue to practice

00:56:23.240 --> 00:56:24.930
and have the school accommodate,

00:56:24.930 --> 00:56:26.500
have the patients accommodate.

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:28.390
I think when you have a
caregiver in a wheelchair

00:56:28.390 --> 00:56:30.487
there's often a transfer
where people think that

00:56:30.487 --> 00:56:33.620
the doctor has a bigger
problem than the patient does

00:56:33.620 --> 00:56:35.687
and you really have to negotiate that.

00:56:35.687 --> 00:56:37.960
I think that we have a lot to learn

00:56:37.960 --> 00:56:39.813
and you know I'm excited that,

00:56:39.813 --> 00:56:42.317
that we're talking about that today.

00:56:42.317 --> 00:56:43.150
- May I address.

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:46.260
My husband worked for and
he had a cane like you did,

00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:49.280
for most of his working
career as an oncologist,

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:53.310
and he switched from surgery
to radiation oncology

00:56:53.310 --> 00:56:54.870
because he didn't have the dexterity.

00:56:54.870 --> 00:56:57.370
But he worked for six months
in an electric wheelchair,

00:56:57.370 --> 00:56:58.203
at my hospital,

00:56:58.203 --> 00:57:00.598
and I would go empty his
Foley and give him his meds.

00:57:00.598 --> 00:57:03.990
But his cancer patients
were so receptive to him.

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:05.810
I mean I think it was because they were

00:57:05.810 --> 00:57:08.320
end-of-life patients themselves,

00:57:08.320 --> 00:57:11.340
it was beautiful and then he
got too sick to do it anymore.

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:12.877
But he did not lose his practice,

00:57:12.877 --> 00:57:16.180
and a quadriplegic and he
had to scribe and you know.

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:17.580
- [Attendee] Yeah, very courageous.

00:57:17.580 --> 00:57:18.700
- Yeah.

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:19.550
- More questions?

00:57:22.700 --> 00:57:23.891
- [Attendee] Thank you all so much.

00:57:23.891 --> 00:57:25.680
Sarah, I love what you said about

00:57:25.680 --> 00:57:27.529
sort of making your office obsolete

00:57:27.529 --> 00:57:30.140
and I'm curious if part of that

00:57:30.140 --> 00:57:32.928
is teaching students
the self-advocacy skills

00:57:32.928 --> 00:57:36.530
to kind of know what
they need and ask for it

00:57:36.530 --> 00:57:37.920
and how to do that.

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.560
- Yeah definitely.

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:43.190
I think that can be a really
big hurdle for students

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:45.630
to get over when they
first come as a first year

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:49.130
because the laws that apply
in the K through 12 world

00:57:49.130 --> 00:57:51.630
are very different than the
laws that apply in higher ed.

00:57:51.630 --> 00:57:54.600
And they go through K
through 12 with their parents

00:57:54.600 --> 00:57:56.170
and their teachers and administrators,

00:57:56.170 --> 00:57:58.770
just all knowing what the disability is,

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:00.310
knowing what the accommodations are,

00:58:00.310 --> 00:58:02.250
and everything just kind
of happening for them

00:58:02.250 --> 00:58:04.640
almost magically and then they get here

00:58:04.640 --> 00:58:07.290
and cross some invisible line
that they never knew existed,

00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:10.140
and then suddenly they have
to advocate for themselves,

00:58:10.140 --> 00:58:12.150
and they're in our office crying

00:58:12.150 --> 00:58:15.272
because they have no idea
how any of this works.

00:58:15.272 --> 00:58:18.920
So we try to first off be
very patient and sympathetic

00:58:18.920 --> 00:58:21.200
and understanding with students
when they first get here

00:58:21.200 --> 00:58:23.178
because it's a lot to suddenly

00:58:23.178 --> 00:58:25.440
have to advocate for yourself,

00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:27.170
especially if you've never done it before.

00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:28.990
It's intimidating, it's daunting,

00:58:28.990 --> 00:58:31.187
I had a student break
down and cry one time

00:58:31.187 --> 00:58:33.740
when she understood
that she needed to tell

00:58:33.740 --> 00:58:35.882
her faculty that she used accommodations

00:58:35.882 --> 00:58:37.760
and she just started crying and said

00:58:37.760 --> 00:58:40.180
but he's a Nobel Prize winner,

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:43.993
how can I possibly have
that conversation with him.

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:47.160
So I think that is tough.

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:51.924
And then especially in the
realm of invisible disabilities,

00:58:51.924 --> 00:58:55.850
there can be an extra
layer of difficulty there

00:58:55.850 --> 00:59:00.310
because if you're having
conversations with people,

00:59:00.310 --> 00:59:02.630
and they can't see and understand

00:59:02.630 --> 00:59:04.230
that you're a person with a disability

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:06.760
and they just kind of have
to take your word for it.

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:09.130
Some people have a hard time with that,

00:59:09.130 --> 00:59:12.510
and they really grapple with you know,

00:59:12.510 --> 00:59:17.510
why is bipolar, why would
that be a disability?

00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:19.900
Can't you just toughen up and get over it?

00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:22.570
So being a self-advocate
in the face of that

00:59:22.570 --> 00:59:24.350
can be really hard for anyone,

00:59:24.350 --> 00:59:27.540
and especially a young person
who's also trying to come

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:29.230
to college and learn all the other things

00:59:29.230 --> 00:59:31.060
that 18 year-olds are learning.

00:59:31.060 --> 00:59:33.962
So I do think self-advocacy
is really important

00:59:33.962 --> 00:59:37.780
but I also think that all the onus

00:59:37.780 --> 00:59:40.370
shouldn't be put on the
student to do that, right?

00:59:40.370 --> 00:59:42.950
There needs to also be,
kind of a culture change

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:44.360
in the community at large

00:59:44.360 --> 00:59:46.217
that's more accepting of those things,

00:59:46.217 --> 00:59:47.670
and more understanding of things

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:49.233
even if they can't see it.

00:59:51.440 --> 00:59:52.273
- Anybody else?

00:59:59.310 --> 01:00:01.113
- [Attendee] Hi I was wondering,

01:00:02.660 --> 01:00:05.820
what percentage of the
undergraduate population

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:07.890
and you know, the other schools as well,

01:00:07.890 --> 01:00:10.660
currently identifies as
a person with disability,

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:14.280
both physical or mental health problems?

01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:17.940
And would you consider
that you are an underserved

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:19.670
or under, not underserved necessary,

01:00:19.670 --> 01:00:21.930
but underrepresented minority at Yale?

01:00:21.930 --> 01:00:24.070
And the point that you made about it,

01:00:24.070 --> 01:00:25.584
being a place of perfection,

01:00:25.584 --> 01:00:29.040
is that something that
prevents many students

01:00:29.040 --> 01:00:32.683
who could contribute here
from even arriving on campus

01:00:32.683 --> 01:00:35.893
and contributing in various ways?

01:00:37.129 --> 01:00:40.550
- So we have about 10%
of the student population

01:00:40.550 --> 01:00:42.060
registered with our office.

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:43.920
I don't really know the breakdown

01:00:43.920 --> 01:00:46.430
for undergrad versus all
the professional schools

01:00:46.430 --> 01:00:47.593
off the top of my head.

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:52.253
My sense is that it's about the
same across all the schools.

01:00:53.570 --> 01:00:58.570
That 10% is students who
have a lifetime disability,

01:00:58.930 --> 01:01:00.680
students who are new to a diagnosis,

01:01:00.680 --> 01:01:02.380
like Paige was her first year,

01:01:02.380 --> 01:01:04.359
students who got a
concussion a couple weeks ago

01:01:04.359 --> 01:01:06.020
and are just dealing with that

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:07.860
for the rest of this semester.

01:01:07.860 --> 01:01:09.900
So it's a really wide range of things.

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:13.160
Some use accommodations
throughout their entire time here,

01:01:13.160 --> 01:01:16.470
some students use it just
for a kind of finite period

01:01:16.470 --> 01:01:18.140
of time as they're recovering,

01:01:18.140 --> 01:01:20.560
we have students that have
situations that are sporadic

01:01:20.560 --> 01:01:23.460
and they might be heavy users
of accommodations one semester

01:01:23.460 --> 01:01:25.770
and then not not want
anything to do with us

01:01:25.770 --> 01:01:26.883
for the next year.

01:01:27.880 --> 01:01:30.890
But generally about 10%
of the student population.

01:01:30.890 --> 01:01:33.034
I do think we are under resourced.

01:01:33.034 --> 01:01:33.867
(crowd laughs)

01:01:33.867 --> 01:01:35.440
In this sense that there's
a lot of other work

01:01:35.440 --> 01:01:36.943
that we could do.

01:01:38.090 --> 01:01:41.270
As it is where we're we're
making accommodations

01:01:41.270 --> 01:01:42.660
for things that come up, right?

01:01:42.660 --> 01:01:45.650
So a student's in a class
and they have some material

01:01:45.650 --> 01:01:48.850
that they can't access, so
we're working to make it so that

01:01:48.850 --> 01:01:50.670
they can access that reading.

01:01:50.670 --> 01:01:52.100
If we had more resources,

01:01:52.100 --> 01:01:54.500
we could do more front-end work

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:56.449
and work directly with the faculty

01:01:56.449 --> 01:02:00.390
and make sure that all
materials are going to be

01:02:00.390 --> 01:02:03.340
digitally accessible for
all classes moving forward

01:02:03.340 --> 01:02:06.608
so then we don't have to
do those accommodations,

01:02:06.608 --> 01:02:09.450
case-by-case basis.

01:02:09.450 --> 01:02:12.460
I think I'm trying to put
our office out of work

01:02:12.460 --> 01:02:15.794
a number of times but I do
think that there's a lot of room

01:02:15.794 --> 01:02:20.340
for that kind of extra resources

01:02:20.340 --> 01:02:21.710
could help us do that work better,

01:02:21.710 --> 01:02:24.779
and then also kind of to
what Paige has talked about,

01:02:24.779 --> 01:02:29.065
there's a lot of community
building that needs to happen.

01:02:29.065 --> 01:02:32.490
Our office is kind of more for compliance

01:02:32.490 --> 01:02:36.090
so I'm not sure exactly where
the right fit is for that,

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:38.670
but I do think there's a real need for,

01:02:38.670 --> 01:02:41.420
and a value in building a community

01:02:41.420 --> 01:02:42.870
at the university generally,

01:02:42.870 --> 01:02:44.490
and there's a lot of
room for growth there.

01:02:44.490 --> 01:02:46.530
- [Janni] Sarah could you take
that just one step further

01:02:46.530 --> 01:02:47.790
and the elephant in the room,

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:49.630
we really haven't talked a lot about.

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:52.019
Is mental health and mental illness.

01:02:52.019 --> 01:02:57.019
We all know that suicide
is a crisis situation

01:02:57.480 --> 01:03:00.980
in all campuses across the
nation and around the world.

01:03:00.980 --> 01:03:04.850
And I have heard tell some
peer support groups hearing

01:03:04.850 --> 01:03:05.940
at other universities.

01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:08.650
Would you like to comment
on what your department

01:03:08.650 --> 01:03:11.215
does in terms of helping
students in that crisis?

01:03:11.215 --> 01:03:14.740
- So we're not mental health clinicians

01:03:15.654 --> 01:03:18.020
and we like to make that very clear right?

01:03:18.020 --> 01:03:19.920
We are not the kind of first stop.

01:03:19.920 --> 01:03:22.090
If you're in a crisis,

01:03:22.090 --> 01:03:24.080
then we will help you
get to the right people,

01:03:24.080 --> 01:03:26.960
we'll help you get connected
with student mental health.

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:30.513
But that's not what what
we ought to be doing.

01:03:31.550 --> 01:03:33.100
We do, are by far,

01:03:33.100 --> 01:03:36.470
our fastest-growing kind
of percentage of students,

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:39.473
is students that identify
with mental health concerns.

01:03:40.574 --> 01:03:44.557
And we are continually working
on building the community

01:03:46.217 --> 01:03:49.010
and the relationship with
student mental health.

01:03:49.010 --> 01:03:50.550
There's a number of student groups,

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:52.550
Paige would probably be
better equipped to talk about

01:03:52.550 --> 01:03:55.730
this student groups that work
on issues around mental health

01:03:55.730 --> 01:03:58.930
and peer counseling and just
kind of raising awareness

01:03:58.930 --> 01:04:00.640
about mental health on campus generally

01:04:00.640 --> 01:04:03.080
and I think that's really
important work also.

01:04:03.080 --> 01:04:05.360
- Yeah so before I talk
about mental health,

01:04:05.360 --> 01:04:07.810
I want to touch briefly
on the original question.

01:04:07.810 --> 01:04:10.610
I just want to add that
not everyone who identifies

01:04:10.610 --> 01:04:13.260
with a disability or who
requires accommodations

01:04:13.260 --> 01:04:15.143
goes to student accessibility services.

01:04:15.143 --> 01:04:19.180
A couple of, even my peer
mentees alone not even,

01:04:19.180 --> 01:04:20.990
just like, looking at the whole program,

01:04:20.990 --> 01:04:23.150
but just the ones who are my mentees,

01:04:23.150 --> 01:04:25.716
have not been registered with
student accessibility services

01:04:25.716 --> 01:04:28.160
and I've you know,
suggested that they register

01:04:28.160 --> 01:04:29.310
and seek accommodations,

01:04:29.310 --> 01:04:32.350
but not everyone with a
disability on this campus does.

01:04:32.350 --> 01:04:36.228
So that 10% number it's
probably much higher in reality

01:04:36.228 --> 01:04:38.030
in terms of how many students

01:04:38.030 --> 01:04:39.520
need some form of accommodation,

01:04:39.520 --> 01:04:41.530
have some form of disability.

01:04:41.530 --> 01:04:43.110
I would also add that,

01:04:43.110 --> 01:04:45.550
we also feel like student
accessibility services

01:04:45.550 --> 01:04:47.500
does not have enough resources,

01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:50.580
and we feel that as a community
we are underrepresented

01:04:50.580 --> 01:04:53.130
on this campus in terms of you know,

01:04:53.130 --> 01:04:55.430
professor's hearing our voices,

01:04:55.430 --> 01:04:57.730
student groups having our involvement

01:04:57.730 --> 01:04:59.250
or requesting our involvement,

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:02.060
or even taking into
consideration the accessibility

01:05:02.060 --> 01:05:04.060
of the location of an event.

01:05:04.060 --> 01:05:06.280
We put a section in the guide

01:05:06.280 --> 01:05:07.560
for navigating disability at Yale,

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:10.280
that talks about social life on campus.

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:13.960
And you know, our
secretary of events, Aria,

01:05:13.960 --> 01:05:16.330
she's fantastic, she is in a wheelchair

01:05:16.330 --> 01:05:18.130
and she was talking
about the accessibility

01:05:18.130 --> 01:05:21.194
of different frats only
one frat on this campus

01:05:21.194 --> 01:05:23.562
is wheelchair accessible, for example.

01:05:23.562 --> 01:05:25.680
And the fact that that you know,

01:05:25.680 --> 01:05:29.160
came into conversation in our
group is just very interesting

01:05:29.160 --> 01:05:31.640
that you know, none of these
fraternities sororities have

01:05:31.640 --> 01:05:34.130
thought about accessibility
of their locations.

01:05:34.130 --> 01:05:36.894
A lot of these social groups
that occur off campus,

01:05:36.894 --> 01:05:39.270
a lot of the events that occur off campus,

01:05:39.270 --> 01:05:40.820
are not in accessible locations

01:05:40.820 --> 01:05:42.470
and just the lack of involvement

01:05:42.470 --> 01:05:46.840
and requesting of disabled
voices in some of those areas,

01:05:46.840 --> 01:05:49.580
has made it so we can't
even enter those spaces.

01:05:49.580 --> 01:05:51.930
So I think that you know, we're generally,

01:05:51.930 --> 01:05:54.470
our voices are underrepresented
in certain sections

01:05:54.470 --> 01:05:56.080
of the student population,

01:05:56.080 --> 01:05:57.290
not across the board,

01:05:57.290 --> 01:05:59.260
but there are certain
areas where you know,

01:05:59.260 --> 01:06:01.840
the community could do better
in terms of listening to us

01:06:01.840 --> 01:06:03.520
and having us involved.

01:06:03.520 --> 01:06:05.980
And then to the point about mental health.

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:08.840
We as DEFY have partner with
a couple mental health groups

01:06:08.840 --> 01:06:13.840
on campus so the Yale layer
is a publication that deals

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:16.150
with mental health and
mental illness on this campus

01:06:16.150 --> 01:06:18.020
and we have worked with them.

01:06:18.020 --> 01:06:19.820
We've gotten involved a little bit with,

01:06:19.820 --> 01:06:21.410
formerly known as Mind Matters,

01:06:21.410 --> 01:06:23.970
I don't know what their new name is,

01:06:23.970 --> 01:06:25.250
but they're a group that promotes

01:06:25.250 --> 01:06:27.203
mental well-being on campus.

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:30.120
You know we've worked some,

01:06:30.120 --> 01:06:32.190
we've reached out to the
Walden peer counselors,

01:06:32.190 --> 01:06:34.680
they do peer counseling for
students with mental illness

01:06:34.680 --> 01:06:37.400
and we've tried in general
to be more inclusive

01:06:37.400 --> 01:06:38.820
of students with mental illness.

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:41.730
So the peer mentor program
did a bunch of outreach

01:06:41.730 --> 01:06:45.960
to get students with mental
illness more involved

01:06:45.960 --> 01:06:49.010
and we do have quite a few
mentees whose diagnosis

01:06:49.010 --> 01:06:51.221
are you know, mental health related.

01:06:51.221 --> 01:06:54.170
So that is a huge and growing part of our

01:06:54.170 --> 01:06:55.560
undergraduate advocacy community,

01:06:55.560 --> 01:06:57.423
as well as those of mental illness.

01:06:58.429 --> 01:06:59.762
- [Janni] I think we have time
for a couple more questions.

01:06:59.762 --> 01:07:01.595
Is there anybody else?

01:07:03.620 --> 01:07:04.640
- [Mary] Hi my name's Mary,

01:07:04.640 --> 01:07:07.670
I'm a rehabilitation
physician by training,

01:07:07.670 --> 01:07:11.470
but Yale School of Public
Health three years ago.

01:07:11.470 --> 01:07:13.455
I think this was an
excellent seminar today

01:07:13.455 --> 01:07:16.120
and really appreciate the candidness

01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:20.010
of all of our, of our moderator
and all of our speakers.

01:07:20.010 --> 01:07:23.437
So thank you very much for that.

01:07:23.437 --> 01:07:25.200
I think this is a great start.

01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:27.090
I think this is opening
up the conversation

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:28.430
here in the Yale community.

01:07:28.430 --> 01:07:30.020
I think in the state of Connecticut

01:07:30.020 --> 01:07:34.390
and even nationally we
can do so much more.

01:07:35.420 --> 01:07:38.160
Mentioning that next year
is the 30th anniversary

01:07:38.160 --> 01:07:41.445
of the ADA, you know perfect
opportunity I think for Yale.

01:07:41.445 --> 01:07:44.350
Through cross school collaboration,

01:07:44.350 --> 01:07:49.170
to have a longer seminar
to talk about these issues.

01:07:49.170 --> 01:07:50.350
So I'll throw that out there for,

01:07:50.350 --> 01:07:52.680
I would love to participate
in that by the way.

01:07:52.680 --> 01:07:54.340
I work in New Haven now not for Yale,

01:07:54.340 --> 01:07:59.340
but I think it's an
opportunity, thank you.

01:08:00.020 --> 01:08:02.010
- Great idea, thank you.

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:03.643
One last question anybody?

01:08:09.790 --> 01:08:11.590
- [Attendee] Hi good afternoon
thank you so much for that.

01:08:11.590 --> 01:08:12.840
That discussion it was fantastic.

01:08:12.840 --> 01:08:14.780
I like my neighbor was really impressed

01:08:14.780 --> 01:08:15.760
by what you said Sara.

01:08:15.760 --> 01:08:20.760
About you know, a world where
your office is not needed.

01:08:21.030 --> 01:08:24.060
And so when I think about
destigmatizing disability,

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:25.650
one of the major opportunities,

01:08:25.650 --> 01:08:28.146
I see as a rehabilitation physician,

01:08:28.146 --> 01:08:30.830
is recreation and sports.

01:08:30.830 --> 01:08:32.830
I think there's very
few things that are more

01:08:32.830 --> 01:08:36.420
galvanizing, cohesive,
and completely empowering.

01:08:36.420 --> 01:08:38.210
And so I was just
wondering, in your office,

01:08:38.210 --> 01:08:40.550
how many students who have disabilities

01:08:40.550 --> 01:08:42.660
or who identifies having a disability,

01:08:42.660 --> 01:08:45.860
come to you looking for recreation
opportunities on campus,

01:08:45.860 --> 01:08:48.000
and what are those opportunities?

01:08:48.000 --> 01:08:49.560
And if there aren't that many,

01:08:49.560 --> 01:08:50.820
how do you think that might change?

01:08:50.820 --> 01:08:53.440
And this can go to either Paige or Sarah.

01:08:53.440 --> 01:08:56.910
- So that's an interesting
question because

01:08:56.910 --> 01:08:58.533
in the last year or so,

01:08:58.533 --> 01:09:02.283
I think we've seen that coming
up more and more and more.

01:09:03.410 --> 01:09:05.680
I think that the biggest
way that my office interacts

01:09:05.680 --> 01:09:08.064
with student athletes is after an injury.

01:09:08.064 --> 01:09:09.610
(crowd laughs)

01:09:09.610 --> 01:09:11.799
We have met the entire women's rugby team.

01:09:11.799 --> 01:09:14.050
(laughs)

01:09:14.050 --> 01:09:15.600
So you know I think that,

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:19.020
that there's a good awareness
of what we do generally

01:09:19.020 --> 01:09:21.710
in the student athlete community.

01:09:21.710 --> 01:09:23.350
We've gotten questions recently

01:09:23.350 --> 01:09:25.970
about the accessibility of
the pool at Payne Whitney,

01:09:25.970 --> 01:09:27.330
and things like that.

01:09:27.330 --> 01:09:29.580
I've recently been opening up connections

01:09:29.580 --> 01:09:33.930
with administrators in
the gym to work on issues

01:09:33.930 --> 01:09:35.133
about accessibility.

01:09:36.410 --> 01:09:38.720
I think that there's a
lot of room for growth

01:09:38.720 --> 01:09:40.600
but like so many other things,

01:09:40.600 --> 01:09:41.916
there is definitely,

01:09:41.916 --> 01:09:44.800
all the attitudinal pieces are in place.

01:09:44.800 --> 01:09:48.160
It's just kind of figuring
out how do we make everything

01:09:48.160 --> 01:09:49.750
as accessible as it can be.

01:09:49.750 --> 01:09:51.030
Paige might have more information

01:09:51.030 --> 01:09:52.450
from a student perspective though?

01:09:52.450 --> 01:09:54.550
- Honestly not really.

01:09:54.550 --> 01:09:58.270
I've, you know, I've
noticed a lot of students

01:09:58.270 --> 01:09:59.440
are trying to get more involved,

01:09:59.440 --> 01:10:02.860
especially intramurals
because they're competitive,

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:03.793
but less so.

01:10:05.800 --> 01:10:08.660
That's sort of like a
team bonding experience

01:10:08.660 --> 01:10:10.010
in your residential college,

01:10:10.010 --> 01:10:12.470
and I think that that creates
more community cohesion

01:10:12.470 --> 01:10:15.130
which then makes students
with various disabilities

01:10:15.130 --> 01:10:20.130
more likely to pursue
intramurals as a welcoming space.

01:10:20.250 --> 01:10:21.920
I've also noticed that you know,

01:10:21.920 --> 01:10:24.780
some club sports like
there's clubs swimming

01:10:24.780 --> 01:10:25.613
and things like that,

01:10:25.613 --> 01:10:26.840
are probably more accessible,

01:10:26.840 --> 01:10:30.470
but I haven't seen individuals
going those routes.

01:10:30.470 --> 01:10:33.716
So for the last two years
I was on a club sport.

01:10:33.716 --> 01:10:35.500
I was on the climbing team,

01:10:35.500 --> 01:10:38.030
and I would love to see
something like adaptive climbing,

01:10:38.030 --> 01:10:39.320
because I know that it exists,

01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:42.500
and I would love it if we
could bring it to this campus.

01:10:42.500 --> 01:10:44.040
I think there are a lot of opportunities

01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:46.150
for improvement in that regard,

01:10:46.150 --> 01:10:48.760
and since there are so many
club and intramural sports,

01:10:48.760 --> 01:10:50.630
I think there's a lot of different ways

01:10:50.630 --> 01:10:54.920
in which this campus could have
more inclusive opportunities

01:10:54.920 --> 01:10:56.250
for students with disabilities to

01:10:56.250 --> 01:10:58.643
partake in recreational activities.

01:10:59.547 --> 01:11:00.576
- [Janni] Thank you so much.

01:11:00.576 --> 01:11:02.383
So I think we've got, we're
probably out of time for today.

01:11:02.383 --> 01:11:05.810
I think all of us will be
here for a little while,

01:11:05.810 --> 01:11:08.088
at least in case you have
more questions or comments

01:11:08.088 --> 01:11:10.409
that you would like to share with us.

01:11:10.409 --> 01:11:11.826
I want to take this opportunity

01:11:11.826 --> 01:11:13.454
to thank every one of our panelists,

01:11:13.454 --> 01:11:16.700
to thank Henry for putting
this remarkable group together,

01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:21.700
and maybe leave us all with
a message from my mentor,

01:11:22.920 --> 01:11:24.528
Professor John Morton Blum,

01:11:24.528 --> 01:11:27.470
who may be as famous for his cameo

01:11:27.470 --> 01:11:29.580
on the Woody Allen movie, Zelig,

01:11:29.580 --> 01:11:32.000
as he was for his history courses,

01:11:32.000 --> 01:11:33.980
in 20th century American history.

01:11:33.980 --> 01:11:35.530
When he said, above all,

01:11:35.530 --> 01:11:39.460
think, act, be, in the active tense.

01:11:39.460 --> 01:11:41.229
Thank you so much.

01:11:41.229 --> 01:11:43.979
(crowd applause)

01:11:46.289 --> 01:11:47.469
- You did a great job.

01:11:47.469 --> 01:11:48.302
- Yeah.

01:11:52.836 --> 01:11:55.669
(soft bells tune)

